/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Isaiah chapters 36-40
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("isaiah", [
{n:36, name:"Rabshakeh's Taunt", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 36:20",verse:"Who are they among all the gods of these lands, that have delivered their land out of my hand, that the LORD should deliver Jerusalem out of my hand?"}, facts:["Did you know Rabshakeh stood at the very spot where Isaiah had once met King Ahaz, at the conduit of the upper pool in the highway of the fuller's field (Isaiah 7:3)? God sent a prophet there once; now Assyria sent a bully.","Did you know the 'Syrian language' the officers asked for is Aramaic, the trade language of the ancient Near East? They wanted quiet diplomacy; Rabshakeh wanted a public show, so he shouted in Hebrew instead.","Did you know the offer of two thousand horses was really an insult? Rabshakeh was saying Judah could not even find riders for a gift army, let alone fight one."], questions:[
{q:"In which year of King Hezekiah did Sennacherib come up against the defenced cities of Judah?",options:["The fourteenth year","The tenth year","The twentieth year","The thirty-second year"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:1",verse:"Now it came to pass in the fourteenth year of king Hezekiah, that Sennacherib king of Assyria came up against all the defenced cities of Judah, and took them.",insight:"God dates this crisis precisely, down to the year. He keeps a careful record of the troubles you face too; none of them arrive off His calendar."},
{q:"Who sent Rabshakeh to Jerusalem with a great army?",options:["The king of Assyria","The king of Babylon","The king of Egypt","The king of Moab"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:2",verse:"And the king of Assyria sent Rabshakeh from Lachish to Jerusalem unto king Hezekiah with a great army. And he stood by the conduit of the upper pool in the highway of the fuller’s field.",insight:"Behind Rabshakeh's loud voice stood a real king with a real army. Bullies always have backers; remember that your Defender outranks theirs."},
{q:"From which city did the king of Assyria send Rabshakeh?",options:["Lachish","Nineveh","Samaria","Damascus"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:2",verse:"And the king of Assyria sent Rabshakeh from Lachish to Jerusalem unto king Hezekiah with a great army. And he stood by the conduit of the upper pool in the highway of the fuller’s field.",insight:"Lachish was already under siege when the envoy left, which shows how fast the invasion was moving. Fear spreads quickly, but so does faith when you let it."},
{q:"Which three men came out to meet Rabshakeh?",options:["Eliakim, Shebna, and Joah","Isaiah, Micah, and Hosea","Hezekiah, Eliakim, and Shebna","Tartan, Rabsaris, and Rabshakeh"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:3",verse:"Then came forth unto him Eliakim, Hilkiah’s son, which was over the house, and Shebna the scribe, and Joah, Asaph’s son, the recorder.",insight:"Hezekiah sent his steward, his scribe, and his recorder: order and dignity against intimidation. There is wisdom in meeting pressure with calm, prepared people rather than panic."},
{q:"What proud title did Rabshakeh give his master, the king of Assyria?",options:["The great king","The king of kings","The mighty conqueror","The lord of the nations"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:4",verse:"And Rabshakeh said unto them, Say ye now to Hezekiah, Thus saith the great king, the king of Assyria, What confidence is this wherein thou trustest?",insight:"Assyria called its king 'great,' but there is only one truly Great King. The flattery of earthly power shrinks the moment you remember who sits above it."},
{q:"To what did Rabshakeh compare trusting in Egypt?",options:["A staff of a broken reed","A spider's web in the wind","A rope of sand","A shield made of paper"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:6",verse:"Lo, thou trustest in the staff of this broken reed, on Egypt; whereon if a man lean, it will go into his hand, and pierce it: so is Pharaoh king of Egypt to all that trust in him.",insight:"On this one point the bully was right: leaning on Egypt would pierce Judah's hand. Not every enemy word is false; the wise sift even taunts for truth."},
{q:"What did Rabshakeh claim Hezekiah had taken away?",options:["The high places and altars of the LORD","The gates and walls of Jerusalem","The treasures of the temple","The chariots and horsemen of Judah"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:7",verse:"But if thou say to me, We trust in the LORD our God: is it not he, whose high places and whose altars Hezekiah hath taken away, and said to Judah and to Jerusalem, Ye shall worship before this altar?",insight:"Rabshakeh twisted Hezekiah's reforms into an insult against God, as if tearing down idols angered Him. The enemy still misquotes your obedience to shake your confidence; check the charge against Scripture."},
{q:"How many horses did Rabshakeh offer to give Hezekiah, if he could find riders?",options:["Two thousand","Ten thousand","Five hundred","One thousand"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:8",verse:"Now therefore give pledges, I pray thee, to my master the king of Assyria, and I will give thee two thousand horses, if thou be able on thy part to set riders upon them.",insight:"The offer was mockery disguised as generosity. Some 'help' is meant to humiliate; learn to tell the difference before you accept it."},
{q:"What astonishing claim did Rabshakeh make about the LORD?",options:["That the LORD had told him to go up and destroy the land","That the LORD had fled from Jerusalem","That the LORD was weaker than the gods of Hamath","That the LORD had surrendered to Assyria"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:10",verse:"And am I now come up without the LORD against this land to destroy it? the LORD said unto me, Go up against this land, and destroy it.",insight:"The enemy loves to put words in God's mouth. When someone claims 'God told me' something that contradicts His word, trust the written Word over the loud voice."},
{q:"In what language did Hezekiah's officers ask Rabshakeh to speak?",options:["The Syrian language","The Assyrian language","The Egyptian language","The Chaldean language"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:11",verse:"Then said Eliakim and Shebna and Joah unto Rabshakeh, Speak, I pray thee, unto thy servants in the Syrian language; for we understand it: and speak not to us in the Jews’ language, in the ears of the people that are on the wall.",insight:"They asked for Aramaic, the language of diplomacy, hoping to spare the people on the wall. There are times to keep hard talk private; not every battle needs an audience."},
{q:"Why did the officers ask Rabshakeh not to speak in the Jews' language?",options:["Because the people on the wall were listening","Because the king had forbidden Hebrew","Because they themselves could not understand Hebrew","Because the law required Aramaic in war"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:11",verse:"Then said Eliakim and Shebna and Joah unto Rabshakeh, Speak, I pray thee, unto thy servants in the Syrian language; for we understand it: and speak not to us in the Jews’ language, in the ears of the people that are on the wall.",insight:"They wanted to protect ordinary people from fear. Leaders carry this quiet duty: stand between the threat and the tenderhearted when you can."},
{q:"What dreadful fate did Rabshakeh say awaited the men on the wall in a siege?",options:["They would eat their own dung and drink their own piss","They would be carried away to Egypt in chains","They would be thrown from the wall headlong","They would be sold as slaves to the Chaldeans"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:12",verse:"But Rabshakeh said, Hath my master sent me to thy master and to thee to speak these words? hath he not sent me to the men that sit upon the wall, that they may eat their own dung, and drink their own piss with you?",insight:"Rabshakeh painted starvation in the crudest colors to break their courage. Fear-mongering is an old weapon; God's people answer it with God's promises, not panic."},
{q:"How did Rabshakeh deliver his message after the officers' request?",options:["He stood and cried with a loud voice in the Jews' language","He whispered it only to Eliakim","He wrote it on a tablet and left","He sent it by a runner to the king"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:13",verse:"Then Rabshakeh stood, and cried with a loud voice in the Jews’ language, and said, Hear ye the words of the great king, the king of Assyria.",insight:"He did the exact opposite of what he was asked, loudly and on purpose. Some opponents do not want agreement; they want a crowd. Refuse to give them the reaction they came for."},
{q:"What did Rabshakeh warn the people not to let Hezekiah do?",options:["Deceive them into thinking he could deliver them","Tax them for the war effort","Send their sons to Egypt","Close the gates of the city"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:14",verse:"Thus saith the king, Let not Hezekiah deceive you: for he shall not be able to deliver you.",insight:"The tactic was to separate the people from their king, then from their God. Division is step one in every siege; stay close to godly leadership and closer to God."},
{q:"If they surrendered, what did Rabshakeh promise each man could enjoy?",options:["His own vine, his own fig tree, and the waters of his own cistern","A house in Nineveh and a field in Assyria","Gold from the king's treasury","A place in the Assyrian army"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:16",verse:"Hearken not to Hezekiah: for thus saith the king of Assyria, Make an agreement with me by a present, and come out to me: and eat ye every one of his vine, and every one of his fig tree, and drink ye every one the waters of his own cistern;",insight:"The enemy offered comfort in exchange for surrender, and the comforts were their own stolen goods dressed up as gifts. Never trade your freedom for a bribe wrapped in your own blessings."},
{q:"What kind of land did Rabshakeh promise to take them to?",options:["A land like their own, of corn and wine, of bread and vineyards","A land of gold and silver beyond the sea","A land where no one would ever work again","A land of iron and brass for building"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:17",verse:"Until I come and take you away to a land like your own land, a land of corn and wine, a land of bread and vineyards.",insight:"He promised exile would feel like home. It never does. The world always promises that leaving God's place will be painless; the promise is the lie."},
{q:"What challenge did Rabshakeh lay down about the gods of the nations?",options:["That none of them had delivered his land from Assyria","That they were all stronger than the LORD","That they had all joined Assyria willingly","That they had never truly been worshipped"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:18",verse:"Beware lest Hezekiah persuade you, saying, The LORD will deliver us. Hath any of the gods of the nations delivered his land out of the hand of the king of Assyria?",insight:"Every god he named had failed, and he used their failure as proof against the LORD. Past victories over false gods prove nothing about the true God; do not let someone else's idols define your faith."},
{q:"Which peoples' gods did Rabshakeh mock by name?",options:["Hamath, Arphad, and Sepharvaim","Edom, Moab, and Ammon","Tyre, Sidon, and Byblos","Elam, Media, and Persia"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:19",verse:"Where are the gods of Hamath and Arphad? where are the gods of Sepharvaim? and have they delivered Samaria out of my hand?",insight:"He listed real fallen cities to make resistance look hopeless. Evidence of past defeats is real, but it is not the whole story when the living God is still in it."},
{q:"Why did the people answer Rabshakeh not a word?",options:["Because the king's commandment was, Answer him not","Because they could not understand his language","Because they had all fled from the wall","Because Eliakim had told them to laugh at him"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:21",verse:"But they held their peace, and answered him not a word: for the king’s commandment was, saying, Answer him not.",insight:"Hezekiah commanded silence, and silence was strategy. You do not owe every accuser a reply; sometimes the godliest answer is none at all."},
{q:"In what condition did Hezekiah's officers return to him with Rabshakeh's words?",options:["With their clothes rent","With crowns on their heads","With gifts from Assyria","With their swords drawn"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 36:22",verse:"Then came Eliakim, the son of Hilkiah, that was over the household, and Shebna the scribe, and Joah, the son of Asaph, the recorder, to Hezekiah with their clothes rent, and told him the words of Rabshakeh.",insight:"They tore their clothes in grief, not in despair; then they told the king everything. Bring the whole ugly report to the right ears; honesty is the first step toward help."}
]},
{n:37, name:"Hezekiah's Prayer", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 37:35",verse:"For I will defend this city to save it for mine own sake, and for my servant David’s sake."}, facts:["Did you know Sennacherib's own palace inscriptions boast that he trapped Hezekiah in Jerusalem 'like a bird in a cage,' yet never claim to have taken the city? The Assyrian records quietly agree with the Bible's ending.","Did you know the 'blast' God promised may describe a plague or panic that swept the Assyrian camp overnight? Whatever the means, 185,000 soldiers did not wake up in the morning.","Did you know Hezekiah's prayer is one of the longest recorded prayers of a king in Scripture? He spreads the enemy's letter before the LORD like evidence in a courtroom, and God rules in his favor."], questions:[
{q:"What did Hezekiah do when he heard Rabshakeh's words?",options:["He rent his clothes, covered himself with sackcloth, and went into the house of the LORD","He called his army to the wall at once","He sent tribute to Sennacherib immediately","He fled Jerusalem by night"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:1",verse:"And it came to pass, when king Hezekiah heard it, that he rent his clothes, and covered himself with sackcloth, and went into the house of the LORD.",insight:"Hezekiah's first move was not military but spiritual: grief, humility, and the house of God. When bad news lands, the order of your response reveals the anchor of your trust."},
{q:"Whom did Hezekiah send to Isaiah the prophet?",options:["Eliakim, Shebna, and the elders of the priests, covered with sackcloth","His captains of war with a thousand men","The treasurers with gold and silver","His sons to learn the law"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:2",verse:"And he sent Eliakim, who was over the household, and Shebna the scribe, and the elders of the priests covered with sackcloth, unto Isaiah the prophet the son of Amoz.",insight:"He sent his top officials to a prophet, not to a general. Wise leaders ask God for counsel before they ask men for plans."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah describe that day to Isaiah?",options:["A day of trouble, and of rebuke, and of blasphemy","A day of feasting and gladness","A day like any other day","A day of harvest and plenty"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:3",verse:"And they said unto him, Thus saith Hezekiah, This day is a day of trouble, and of rebuke, and of blasphemy: for the children are come to the birth, and there is not strength to bring forth.",insight:"He named the day honestly: trouble, rebuke, blasphemy. God does not require polished prayers; He welcomes the truth about how bad it feels."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah ask Isaiah to do for the remnant that was left?",options:["Lift up his prayer for them","Number them for battle","Hide them in the caves","Lead them out of the city"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:4",verse:"It may be the LORD thy God will hear the words of Rabshakeh, whom the king of Assyria his master hath sent to reproach the living God, and will reprove the words which the LORD thy God hath heard: wherefore lift up thy prayer for the remnant that is left.",insight:"'The remnant that is left' shows Hezekiah knew how small they were. Praying for a remnant is still praying with hope; God has always worked through the few."},
{q:"What was Isaiah's first word from the LORD to Hezekiah?",options:["Be not afraid of the words thou hast heard","Flee the city before morning","Pay the tribute and be free","Trust in Egypt for horses"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:6",verse:"And Isaiah said unto them, Thus shall ye say unto your master, Thus saith the LORD, Be not afraid of the words that thou hast heard, wherewith the servants of the king of Assyria have blasphemed me.",insight:"God's answer began with comfort, not strategy: do not be afraid. Courage is often God's first gift before He shows His plan."},
{q:"What did God say He would send upon Sennacherib?",options:["A blast, a rumour, and a sword in his own land","A famine in Assyria","A flood at Nineveh","A plague upon his horses"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:7",verse:"Behold, I will send a blast upon him, and he shall hear a rumour, and return to his own land; and I will cause him to fall by the sword in his own land.",insight:"God promised three strokes: a blast, a rumour, and death at home. He fights with means we cannot see; your part is to trust the God who works behind the scenes."},
{q:"When Rabshakeh returned, where did he find the king of Assyria fighting?",options:["Against Libnah, for he had departed from Lachish","Against Jerusalem itself","Against the city of Samaria","Against the land of Egypt"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:8",verse:"So Rabshakeh returned, and found the king of Assyria warring against Libnah: for he had heard that he was departed from Lachish.",insight:"The great invasion was already shifting ground; Assyria's plans were moving even as they boasted. Enemy schemes are never as settled as they sound."},
{q:"Which king of Ethiopia was reported to be coming to make war with Sennacherib?",options:["Tirhakah","Candace","Zerah","Necho"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:9",verse:"And he heard say concerning Tirhakah king of Ethiopia, He is come forth to make war with thee. And when he heard it, he sent messengers to Hezekiah, saying,",insight:"God raised a distraction on Assyria's flank. Sometimes deliverance comes through trouble elsewhere; God has more pieces on the board than you can see."},
{q:"What did Sennacherib's letter warn Hezekiah not to believe?",options:["That his God would keep Jerusalem from Assyria's hand","That Isaiah was a true prophet","That Egypt would send an army","That the wall could hold forever"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:10",verse:"Thus shall ye speak to Hezekiah king of Judah, saying, Let not thy God, in whom thou trustest, deceive thee, saying, Jerusalem shall not be given into the hand of the king of Assyria.",insight:"The letter attacked Hezekiah's trust directly: 'Let not thy God deceive thee.' When pressure targets your confidence in God, recognize the oldest trick in the book."},
{q:"Which places did the letter boast Assyria had utterly destroyed?",options:["Gozan, Haran, Rezeph, and the children of Eden in Telassar","Tyre, Sidon, and Gebal","Memphis, Thebes, and Zoan","Sodom, Gomorrah, and Zoar"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:12",verse:"Have the gods of the nations delivered them which my fathers have destroyed, as Gozan, and Haran, and Rezeph, and the children of Eden which were in Telassar?",insight:"The letter listed real conquests to make the threat feel certain. Facts can be true and still not be the final word; Assyria's record did not include the living God."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah do with Sennacherib's letter?",options:["He spread it before the LORD in His house","He burned it in the fire","He hid it in the treasury","He sent it back unopened"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:14",verse:"And Hezekiah received the letter from the hand of the messengers, and read it: and Hezekiah went up unto the house of the LORD, and spread it before the LORD.",insight:"He laid the threat out before God like a case file. Bring the actual letter, the real bill, the true diagnosis; God wants the specifics, not a summary."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah address God at the start of his prayer?",options:["O LORD of hosts, God of Israel, that dwellest between the cherubims","O God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob only","O King of heaven, far above us","O Judge of the earth, hear my plea"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:16",verse:"O LORD of hosts, God of Israel, that dwellest between the cherubims, thou art the God, even thou alone, of all the kingdoms of the earth: thou hast made heaven and earth.",insight:"He began with who God is: enthroned, unique, Creator of heaven and earth. Prayer that starts with God's greatness ends with quieter fears."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah say about the gods Assyria had cast into the fire?",options:["They were no gods, but the work of men's hands, wood and stone","They were mighty but defeated","They had abandoned their peoples","They were hidden by their priests"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:19",verse:"And have cast their gods into the fire: for they were no gods, but the work of men’s hands, wood and stone: therefore they have destroyed them.",insight:"Hezekiah saw through the idols: wood and stone cannot save. Naming what is false clears the way to trust what is true."},
{q:"Why did Hezekiah ask God to save them from Sennacherib's hand?",options:["That all the kingdoms of the earth may know that He is the LORD alone","That Judah might become the greatest nation","That the temple treasures might be spared","That his own name might be remembered"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:20",verse:"Now therefore, O LORD our God, save us from his hand, that all the kingdoms of the earth may know that thou art the LORD, even thou only.",insight:"His motive was God's glory among the nations, not just Judah's comfort. Prayers aimed at God's fame carry a weight that self-centered prayers do not."},
{q:"What did the LORD say the virgin daughter of Zion had done to Sennacherib?",options:["Despised him, laughed him to scorn, and shaken her head at him","Wept and fled before him","Paid him tribute in secret","Opened her gates to him"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:22",verse:"This is the word which the LORD hath spoken concerning him; The virgin, the daughter of Zion, hath despised thee, and laughed thee to scorn; the daughter of Jerusalem hath shaken her head at thee.",insight:"God pictured Jerusalem as a young woman laughing at the bully. Heaven sees intimidation differently than we do; what terrifies us can be laughable to God."},
{q:"Against whom had Sennacherib really exalted his voice?",options:["The Holy One of Israel","The king of Judah","The gods of the nations","The priests of the temple"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:23",verse:"Whom hast thou reproached and blasphemed? and against whom hast thou exalted thy voice, and lifted up thine eyes on high? even against the Holy One of Israel.",insight:"His quarrel was never really with Hezekiah; it was with God. When you are attacked for standing with the Lord, remember who the true target is."},
{q:"What did God promise to put in Sennacherib?",options:["His hook in his nose and His bridle in his lips, and turn him back","A crown of shame upon his head","Chains upon his hands and feet","A mark upon his forehead"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:29",verse:"Because thy rage against me, and thy tumult, is come up into mine ears, therefore will I put my hook in thy nose, and my bridle in thy lips, and I will turn thee back by the way by which thou camest.",insight:"God would lead the great king home like a captured animal. No ruler is too proud to be turned; God holds the bridle of every tyrant."},
{q:"What was the sign God gave Hezekiah about the coming years?",options:["They would eat self-sown grain for two years, then sow and reap in the third","The sun would stand still for a whole day","Rain would fall for forty days","The dead would rise in Jerusalem"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:30",verse:"And this shall be a sign unto thee, Ye shall eat this year such as groweth of itself; and the second year that which springeth of the same: and in the third year sow ye, and reap, and plant vineyards, and eat the fruit thereof.",insight:"God promised food from what He caused to grow, then a return to normal sowing. Recovery has stages; God provides for each one, even the in-between years."},
{q:"How many Assyrians did the angel of the LORD smite in the camp in one night?",options:["A hundred and fourscore and five thousand","Ten thousand","Fifty thousand","A thousand"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:36",verse:"Then the angel of the LORD went forth, and smote in the camp of the Assyrians a hundred and fourscore and five thousand: and when they arose early in the morning, behold, they were all dead corpses.",insight:"185,000 died while Jerusalem slept. The battle was won without a single Jewish sword drawn; some victories are God's alone, and He asks only that you watch and trust."},
{q:"How did Sennacherib die in his own land?",options:["His sons Adrammelech and Sharezer slew him with the sword as he worshipped Nisroch","He died of plague in his palace","His army rose up against him","He fell from his chariot"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 37:38",verse:"And it came to pass, as he was worshipping in the house of Nisroch his god, that Adrammelech and Sharezer his sons smote him with the sword; and they escaped into the land of Armenia: and Esar-haddon his son reigned in his stead.",insight:"He died bowing to a god of wood and stone, killed by his own sons, exactly as God had said. Boasting against the living God ends where it deserves; the word of the LORD never falls to the ground."}
]},
{n:38, name:"Hezekiah's Sickness", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 38:7",verse:"And this shall be a sign unto thee from the LORD, that the LORD will do this thing that he hath spoken;"}, facts:["Did you know the sundial of Ahaz is one of the earliest mentions of a timekeeping device in Scripture? God turned its shadow backward ten degrees, a sign no man could fake and no idol could copy.","Did you know Hezekiah's song in verses 10 to 20 is the only full thanksgiving psalm of a king of Judah outside the book of Psalms? He turned his sickbed into a sanctuary.","Did you know figs were a common ancient poultice for boils? God answered Hezekiah's tears with both a miracle and ordinary medicine, and there is no shame in using the means He provides."], questions:[
{q:"What message did Isaiah bring to the sick Hezekiah?",options:["Set thine house in order: for thou shalt die, and not live","Flee to Egypt for physicians","Offer a thousand burnt offerings","Call the elders to anoint thee"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:1",verse:"In those days was Hezekiah sick unto death. And Isaiah the prophet the son of Amoz came unto him, and said unto him, Thus saith the LORD, Set thine house in order: for thou shalt die, and not live.",insight:"God's word was blunt: prepare to die. Sometimes the kindest thing God does is tell us the truth plainly, because only truth can ready a heart."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah do when he heard he would die?",options:["He turned his face toward the wall and prayed unto the LORD","He called his army commanders together","He wrote his will and sealed it","He sent for Isaiah to argue"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:2",verse:"Then Hezekiah turned his face toward the wall, and prayed unto the LORD,",insight:"He turned his face to the wall, shutting out the world to face God. In crisis, turn away from the noise and toward the One who hears."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah ask the LORD to remember in his prayer?",options:["How he had walked before Him in truth and with a perfect heart","How many battles he had won","How much gold he had given the temple","How long he had reigned"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:3",verse:"And said, Remember now, O LORD, I beseech thee, how I have walked before thee in truth and with a perfect heart, and have done that which is good in thy sight. And Hezekiah wept sore.",insight:"He pleaded a life of sincere obedience, not perfection. A 'perfect heart' means a whole, undivided one; God honors a life aimed at Him even when it stumbles."},
{q:"How many years did God add to Hezekiah's life?",options:["Fifteen years","Ten years","Twenty years","Seven years"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:5",verse:"Go, and say to Hezekiah, Thus saith the LORD, the God of David thy father, I have heard thy prayer, I have seen thy tears: behold, I will add unto thy days fifteen years.",insight:"Fifteen years: a measured, specific gift. God answers prayer with particulars, not vagueness; He knows exactly what you need and gives it by name."},
{q:"Besides longer life, what did God promise Hezekiah?",options:["To deliver him and Jerusalem from the king of Assyria","To give him a son to succeed him","To make him king over all nations","To remove all sickness from Judah"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:6",verse:"And I will deliver thee and this city out of the hand of the king of Assyria: and I will defend this city.",insight:"The promise covered both the man and the city; personal mercy and public deliverance came together. God's care for you is never separated from His care for His people."},
{q:"What sign did God give that He would keep His word?",options:["The shadow on the sundial of Ahaz went ten degrees backward","The temple veil was torn in two","Fire fell from heaven at noon","The Jordan River stood still"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:8",verse:"Behold, I will bring again the shadow of the degrees, which is gone down in the sun dial of Ahaz, ten degrees backward. So the sun returned ten degrees, by which degrees it was gone down.",insight:"God moved the sun's shadow backward, reversing time's arrow as a pledge. When God gives a sign, it is always bigger than our asking, because His faithfulness is bigger than our doubt."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah write after he recovered?",options:["A song of thanksgiving for his sickness and healing","A letter to the king of Babylon","A new law for the priests","A chronicle of the Assyrian war"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:9",verse:"The writing of Hezekiah king of Judah, when he had been sick, and was recovered of his sickness:",insight:"He wrote it down: the sickness, the prayer, the healing. Recording God's mercies turns a memory into a monument; write down what He does for you."},
{q:"In his song, where did Hezekiah say he would go in the cutting off of his days?",options:["To the gates of the grave","To the land of Egypt","To the wilderness of Zin","To the cities of refuge"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:10",verse:"I said in the cutting off of my days, I shall go to the gates of the grave: I am deprived of the residue of my years.",insight:"He felt his years cut short at the gate of death. Facing the end clarifies what matters; Hezekiah's grief shows that loving life is not a lack of faith."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah fear he would no longer see?",options:["The LORD in the land of the living","The temple in its glory","His son upon the throne","The walls of Jerusalem"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:11",verse:"I said, I shall not see the LORD, even the LORD, in the land of the living: I shall behold man no more with the inhabitants of the world.",insight:"His deepest loss was not life itself but seeing the LORD among the living. The truest grief of dying, for a believer, is the pause in worship; and God answered even that."},
{q:"To what did Hezekiah compare his departing life?",options:["A shepherd's tent taken down, and a weaver's cloth cut off","A ship sinking in the sea","A lamp going out at midnight","A tree cut down in winter"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:12",verse:"Mine age is departed, and is removed from me as a shepherd’s tent: I have cut off like a weaver my life: he will cut me off with pining sickness: from day even to night wilt thou make an end of me.",insight:"A tent struck, a weaving cut: life is temporary and unfinished by our measure. Seeing life's brevity clearly is what makes us number our days wisely."},
{q:"Like what fierce animal did Hezekiah say his pain would break his bones?",options:["A lion","A bear","A wolf","A leopard"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:13",verse:"I reckoned till morning, that, as a lion, so will he break all my bones: from day even to night wilt thou make an end of me.",insight:"He described his suffering honestly, as being mauled by a lion. God can handle graphic honesty about pain; He never asks you to pretty it up."},
{q:"Which gentle bird did Hezekiah say he mourned like?",options:["A dove","A sparrow","A swallow","A crane"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:14",verse:"Like a crane or a swallow, so did I chatter: I did mourn as a dove: mine eyes fail with looking upward: O LORD, I am oppressed; undertake for me.",insight:"He chattered like a swallow and mourned as a dove: restless, then sorrowful. Grief has its own sounds, and God listens to every one of them."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah say he would do all his remaining years?",options:["Go softly in the bitterness of his soul","Sing loudly in the temple courts","Travel to the ends of the earth","Forget the days of his sickness"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:15",verse:"What shall I say? he hath both spoken unto me, and himself hath done it: I shall go softly all my years in the bitterness of my soul.",insight:"Healing did not erase the memory; he would walk softly, humbled. Some lessons stay with us on purpose, keeping us tender long after the pain passes."},
{q:"What had God done with all of Hezekiah's sins?",options:["Cast them all behind His back","Written them in a book","Hidden them under the altar","Washed them in the laver"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:17",verse:"Behold, for peace I had great bitterness: but thou hast in love to my soul delivered it from the pit of corruption: for thou hast cast all my sins behind thy back.",insight:"Behind His back: God deliberately puts forgiven sin where He will not look. If God has cast it behind Him, you can stop dragging it back in front of you."},
{q:"According to Hezekiah's song, who cannot praise God?",options:["The grave and the dead that go down into the pit","The kings of the earth","The priests of the temple","The children of Israel"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:18",verse:"For the grave cannot praise thee, death can not celebrate thee: they that go down into the pit cannot hope for thy truth.",insight:"The dead cannot praise Him, so the living must. Every breath is a remaining chance to worship; do not waste today's praise."},
{q:"Who did Hezekiah say would make known God's truth to the children?",options:["The father","The prophet","The priest","The king"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:19",verse:"The living, the living, he shall praise thee, as I do this day: the father to the children shall make known thy truth.",insight:"Fathers carry the duty of telling the next generation. Your children will learn God's truth first from your mouth; do not outsource the telling."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah say they would celebrate God's saving?",options:["Sing songs to stringed instruments all their days in the house of the LORD","Offer a thousand rams on the altar","Build a new gate for the temple","Keep a feast for seven days"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:20",verse:"The LORD was ready to save me: therefore we will sing my songs to the stringed instruments all the days of our life in the house of the LORD.",insight:"His answer to mercy was lifelong music in God's house. Gratitude that fades in a week is thin; aim for praise that lasts all your days."},
{q:"What remedy did Isaiah order for Hezekiah's boil?",options:["A lump of figs laid as a plaster upon the boil","Oil and wine poured upon it","A cloth dipped in hyssop","Water from the pool of Siloam"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:21",verse:"For Isaiah had said, Let them take a lump of figs, and lay it for a plaister upon the boil, and he shall recover.",insight:"God's miracle came through a poultice of figs. Do not despise simple means; God often wraps His wonders in ordinary things."},
{q:"What sign did Hezekiah ask for?",options:["A sign that he would go up to the house of the LORD","A sign in the sun and moon","A sign that Assyria would fall","A sign for the priests to see"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:22",verse:"Hezekiah also had said, What is the sign that I shall go up to the house of the LORD?",insight:"After all God promised, Hezekiah still asked for a sign, and God gave one. Asking for assurance is not unbelief; God is patient with hearts that want to be sure."},
{q:"What happened right after Hezekiah wept sore before the LORD?",options:["The word of the LORD came to Isaiah with an answer","Isaiah left the city in anger","The priests began to mourn with him","A prophet from Samaria arrived"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 38:4",verse:"Then came the word of the LORD to Isaiah, saying,",insight:"Before Isaiah had even left, God answered. Some prayers do not wait; heaven can respond while the tears are still wet."}
]},
{n:39, name:"The Babylonian Envoys", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 39:6",verse:"Behold, the days come, that all that is in thine house, and that which thy fathers have laid up in store until this day, shall be carried to Babylon: nothing shall be left, saith the LORD."}, facts:["Did you know Merodach-baladan was a real king of Babylon named in Babylonian chronicles? His friendly embassy was almost certainly seeking an ally against Assyria, which is why Hezekiah's show of wealth was a political blunder, not just pride."], questions:[
{q:"Who sent letters and a present to Hezekiah?",options:["Merodach-baladan, king of Babylon","Tirhakah, king of Ethiopia","Necho, king of Egypt","Esar-haddon, king of Assyria"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 39:1",verse:"At that time Merodach-baladan, the son of Baladan, king of Babylon, sent letters and a present to Hezekiah: for he had heard that he had been sick, and was recovered.",insight:"Babylon's king sent gifts, but gifts from rising powers are rarely free. Discernment looks past the wrapping to the motive."},
{q:"Why did the king of Babylon send to Hezekiah at that time?",options:["He had heard that Hezekiah had been sick and was recovered","He wanted Hezekiah's daughter as a bride","He sought timber from Lebanon","He feared an attack from Judah"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 39:1",verse:"At that time Merodach-baladan, the son of Baladan, king of Babylon, sent letters and a present to Hezekiah: for he had heard that he had been sick, and was recovered.",insight:"The embassy came because of the healing, which means God's mercy was being watched. Your testimony is always being observed; steward it carefully."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah show the Babylonian envoys?",options:["His silver, gold, spices, precious ointment, armour, and all his treasures","Only the temple and its courts","The sickbed where he had lain","The walls and gates of Jerusalem"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 39:2",verse:"And Hezekiah was glad of them, and shewed them the house of his precious things, the silver, and the gold, and the spices, and the precious ointment, and all the house of his armour, and all that was found in his treasures: there was nothing in his house, nor in all his dominion, that Hezekiah shewed them not.",insight:"He showed them everything, holding nothing back. Openness is a virtue, but wisdom knows what to keep covered; not every visitor deserves the full tour."},
{q:"How much of his house and dominion did Hezekiah keep hidden from them?",options:["Nothing; there was nothing he did not show them","Half of his treasures","The armoury alone","The temple vessels"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 39:2",verse:"And Hezekiah was glad of them, and shewed them the house of his precious things, the silver, and the gold, and the spices, and the precious ointment, and all the house of his armour, and all that was found in his treasures: there was nothing in his house, nor in all his dominion, that Hezekiah shewed them not.",insight:"'Nothing... that Hezekiah shewed them not' is a chilling sentence. Total transparency with the wrong audience is not honesty; it is carelessness."},
{q:"When Isaiah asked where the men had come from, what did Hezekiah answer?",options:["From a far country, even from Babylon","From Egypt with horses","From Assyria with tribute","From Edom with spices"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 39:3",verse:"Then came Isaiah the prophet unto king Hezekiah, and said unto him, What said these men? and from whence came they unto thee? And Hezekiah said, They are come from a far country unto me, even from Babylon.",insight:"Hezekiah answered plainly, unaware of the danger. Honesty after folly does not undo folly; better to seek counsel before acting than to confess after."},
{q:"What had the visitors seen in Hezekiah's house?",options:["All that was in his house; nothing among his treasures was hidden","Only the outer courts","The royal gardens alone","Nothing, for they were blindfolded"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 39:4",verse:"Then said he, What have they seen in thine house? And Hezekiah answered, All that is in mine house have they seen: there is nothing among my treasures that I have not shewed them.",insight:"Isaiah's second question exposed the full extent. Godly counsel asks the hard follow-up; 'what have they seen?' is a question worth asking before every compromise."},
{q:"What did Isaiah prophesy about Hezekiah's treasures?",options:["All would be carried to Babylon, and nothing would be left","They would be doubled within a year","They would be hidden in the earth","They would be given to the poor"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 39:6",verse:"Behold, the days come, that all that is in thine house, and that which thy fathers have laid up in store until this day, shall be carried to Babylon: nothing shall be left, saith the LORD.",insight:"Everything shown would be taken. What we parade before the world, the world may one day claim; hold your treasures loosely and your obedience tightly."},
{q:"What did Isaiah say would happen to Hezekiah's sons?",options:["They would be taken away as eunuchs in Babylon's palace","They would reign over Babylon","They would flee to Egypt","They would become priests in Jerusalem"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 39:7",verse:"And of thy sons that shall issue from thee, which thou shalt beget, shall they take away; and they shall be eunuchs in the palace of the king of Babylon.",insight:"The consequences reached the next generation. Our choices echo into our children's lives; that is both a warning and a call to choose well."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah respond to Isaiah's hard word?",options:["Good is the word of the LORD; for there shall be peace and truth in my days","He tore his clothes in anger","He refused to listen further","He wept and begged for mercy"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 39:8",verse:"Then said Hezekiah to Isaiah, Good is the word of the LORD which thou hast spoken. He said moreover, For there shall be peace and truth in my days.",insight:"He accepted God's word as good even though it stung, finding comfort that judgment would wait. Submission to God's word, even hard words, is the mark of a heart at peace with Him."},
{q:"With what words did Isaiah begin his message to Hezekiah?",options:["Hear the word of the LORD of hosts","Thus saith the king of Babylon","Behold the folly of thy ways","Woe unto thee, Hezekiah"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 39:5",verse:"Then said Isaiah to Hezekiah, Hear the word of the LORD of hosts:",insight:"Before the rebuke came the summons: hear the word of the LORD. Every correction from God begins with an invitation to listen; the listening is where change starts."}
]},
{n:40, name:"Comfort Ye My People", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 40:29",verse:"He giveth power to the faint; and to them that have no might he increaseth strength."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 40 opens the second great half of the book, turning from judgment to comfort? After 39 chapters of warning, God begins with the words 'Comfort ye, comfort ye.'","Did you know the voice crying in the wilderness in verse 3 is quoted in all four Gospels about John the Baptist? Isaiah saw the forerunner of Christ seven centuries early.","Did you know verse 22 pictures God calling every star by name? Astronomers now catalogue billions of stars, yet Scripture said long ago that not one escapes His counting."], questions:[
{q:"How does Isaiah 40 begin?",options:["Comfort ye, comfort ye my people, saith your God","Hear ye the word of the LORD","Woe unto the rebellious children","Behold, the day of the LORD cometh"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:1",verse:"Comfort ye, comfort ye my people, saith your God.",insight:"God repeats 'comfort' twice, as if once were not enough. When He comforts, He does it thoroughly; receive it fully instead of rationing His kindness."},
{q:"What had Jerusalem received of the LORD's hand for all her sins?",options:["Double","Half the penalty","A single rebuke","Nothing at all"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:2",verse:"Speak ye comfortably to Jerusalem, and cry unto her, that her warfare is accomplished, that her iniquity is pardoned: for she hath received of the LORD’S hand double for all her sins.",insight:"'Double for all her sins' means the punishment was fully paid, even overflowing. God's pardon is not partial; when He forgives, the account is settled."},
{q:"What does the voice in the wilderness cry?",options:["Prepare ye the way of the LORD, make straight in the desert a highway for our God","Flee from the coming wrath","Build a temple in the wilderness","Hide yourselves from the LORD"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:3",verse:"The voice of him that crieth in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the LORD, make straight in the desert a highway for our God.",insight:"Before the King comes, the road is prepared. God still sends voices ahead of His work in your life; listen for the preparation before you expect the arrival."},
{q:"What will happen to every valley and every mountain?",options:["Every valley shall be exalted, and every mountain made low","They shall be removed into the sea","They shall be covered with snow forever","They shall become as the plain of Jordan"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:4",verse:"Every valley shall be exalted, and every mountain and hill shall be made low: and the crooked shall be made straight, and the rough places plain:",insight:"God levels the road: valleys lifted, mountains lowered. Whatever is too low or too high in your life, He is able to make the way straight."},
{q:"To what is all flesh compared in Isaiah 40:6?",options:["Grass, and its beauty to the flower of the field","Dust beneath the feet","Water spilled on the ground","Smoke rising from the fire"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:6",verse:"The voice said, Cry. And he said, What shall I cry? All flesh is grass, and all the goodliness thereof is as the flower of the field:",insight:"Human glory is grass: green today, withered tomorrow. Knowing your frailty is not despair; it is the doorway to trusting what lasts."},
{q:"What shall stand forever?",options:["The word of our God","The mountains of the earth","The kingdoms of men","The temple in Jerusalem"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:8",verse:"The grass withereth, the flower fadeth: but the word of our God shall stand for ever.",insight:"Grass withers and flowers fade, but God's word stands forever. Build your life on the one thing that cannot wither."},
{q:"What message was Zion to lift up to the cities of Judah?",options:["Behold your God","Flee to the mountains","The end is near","Trust in yourselves"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:9",verse:"O Zion, that bringest good tidings, get thee up into the high mountain; O Jerusalem, that bringest good tidings, lift up thy voice with strength; lift it up, be not afraid; say unto the cities of Judah, Behold your God!",insight:"The good tidings had a center: Behold your God. Every true gospel message points away from our efforts and toward Him."},
{q:"How does the LORD care for His flock?",options:["He gathers the lambs with His arm, carries them in His bosom, and gently leads those with young","He drives them with a rod of iron","He leaves them to find their own pasture","He counts them once a year"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:11",verse:"He shall feed his flock like a shepherd: he shall gather the lambs with his arm, and carry them in his bosom, and shall gently lead those that are with young.",insight:"The mighty God of verse 10 is the tender Shepherd of verse 11: strength and gentleness in one Person. You are carried, not merely directed."},
{q:"Who has measured the waters in the hollow of His hand?",options:["The LORD, who meted out heaven with His span","The mightiest of angels","The wisest of kings","No one has ever done it"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:12",verse:"Who hath measured the waters in the hollow of his hand, and meted out heaven with the span, and comprehended the dust of the earth in a measure, and weighed the mountains in scales, and the hills in a balance?",insight:"God holds oceans in His palm and measures skies with His fingers. Your problems are not small to Him, but He is far bigger than your problems."},
{q:"Who has directed the Spirit of the LORD or taught Him as counsellor?",options:["No one","The archangel Michael","The prophets of old","The kings of the earth"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:13",verse:"Who hath directed the Spirit of the LORD, or being his counsellor hath taught him?",insight:"God takes counsel from no one; His wisdom is original. You can trust His plans even when He explains none of them."},
{q:"To what are the nations compared before God?",options:["A drop of a bucket and the small dust of the balance","The sand of the seashore","The stars of the sky","The trees of the forest"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:15",verse:"Behold, the nations are as a drop of a bucket, and are counted as the small dust of the balance: behold, he taketh up the isles as a very little thing.",insight:"All the nations are a drop in a bucket to God. The powers that frighten you are specks to Him; fear them less and trust Him more."},
{q:"What does Isaiah say about Lebanon's forests and beasts as an offering?",options:["They are not sufficient for a burnt offering to God","They would please Him greatly","They are the best offering on earth","They must be offered every morning"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:16",verse:"And Lebanon is not sufficient to burn, nor the beasts thereof sufficient for a burnt offering.",insight:"All Lebanon's wood and wildlife could not fuel a worthy sacrifice. God cannot be bought or impressed; He is to be worshipped, not paid."},
{q:"What question does Isaiah ask about likening God to anything?",options:["To whom will ye liken God, or what likeness compare unto Him","Who can build a house for God","Where shall we find wisdom","When will the LORD return"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:18",verse:"To whom then will ye liken God? or what likeness will ye compare unto him?",insight:"Nothing in creation measures up to the Creator. Every comparison falls short, which is why God must reveal Himself; we could never reason our way up to Him."},
{q:"How is a graven image made, according to verse 19?",options:["The workman melts it, the goldsmith overlays it with gold and casts silver chains","It is carved from a single diamond","It falls from heaven complete","It is woven from fine linen"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:19",verse:"The workman melteth a graven image, and the goldsmith spreadeth it over with gold, and casteth silver chains.",insight:"Idols are manufactured: melted, plated, chained. Anything that needs your craftsmanship to exist cannot save you; worship the Maker, not the made."},
{q:"What does the poor man choose for his idol?",options:["A tree that will not rot, and a workman to carve it so it will not be moved","A stone from the river","A bird of the air","A handful of dust"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:20",verse:"He that is so impoverished that he hath no oblation chooseth a tree that will not rot; he seeketh unto him a cunning workman to prepare a graven image, that shall not be moved.",insight:"Even poverty finds a way to make idols; the heart's factory never closes. Rich or poor, the cure is the same: turn from the made thing to the Maker."},
{q:"What are the inhabitants of the earth like to Him who sits upon its circle?",options:["As grasshoppers","As ants in a hill","As sheep without a shepherd","As dust in the wind"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:22",verse:"It is he that sitteth upon the circle of the earth, and the inhabitants thereof are as grasshoppers; that stretcheth out the heavens as a curtain, and spreadeth them out as a tent to dwell in:",insight:"From God's height we look like grasshoppers, yet He still looks. Smallness is not abandonment; the God above the circle bends down to the dust."},
{q:"What does God do to the princes and judges of the earth?",options:["He brings them to nothing and makes them as vanity","He crowns them forever","He hides them in safety","He makes them as the stars"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:23",verse:"That bringeth the princes to nothing; he maketh the judges of the earth as vanity.",insight:"Earthly power is temporary by divine design. Do not envy the powerful or fear them unduly; their page in history is shorter than they think."},
{q:"What does God do with the host of heaven?",options:["He brings them out by number and calls them all by names","He hides them behind clouds","He numbers them once a year","He lets them wander at will"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:26",verse:"Lift up your eyes on high, and behold who hath created these things, that bringeth out their host by number: he calleth them all by names by the greatness of his might, for that he is strong in power; not one faileth.",insight:"Every star has a name known to God. If He names the stars, He surely knows yours; you are not a number to Him."},
{q:"What is said of the everlasting God, the Creator of the ends of the earth?",options:["He faints not, neither is weary, and His understanding cannot be searched","He rests on the seventh day forever","He grows tired of men's sins","He changes with the seasons"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:28",verse:"Hast thou not known? hast thou not heard, that the everlasting God, the LORD, the Creator of the ends of the earth, fainteth not, neither is weary? there is no searching of his understanding.",insight:"God never tires, never runs out, never needs a rest. When you are weary, you lean on One who is not."},
{q:"What is promised to those who wait upon the LORD?",options:["They shall renew their strength and mount up with wings as eagles","They shall never face trouble again","They shall rule over the nations","They shall live a thousand years"],a:0,ref:"Isaiah 40:31",verse:"But they that wait upon the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings as eagles; they shall run, and not be weary; and they shall walk, and not faint.",insight:"Waiting on God is not wasting time; it is how strength is renewed. Eagles, running, walking: He gives flight for the crisis, endurance for the road, and steadiness for the daily path."}
]}
]);
